\f1\fs22 They looked frailer than in the \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 Candid Criminal footage. Anyway, Gerry had more important targets. He walked into the carpark, among an audience that was larger for the fact that the compound gates had been shut in both directions. Some folks were up there protesting about being trapped while most decided to enjoy the music.\
The band trash-canned their way to the end of a song and was met with applause that thinned out quickly in the evening mountain air.\
Someone in the crowd called out, \'93You will play gospel music.\'94\
The crowd booed, and chanted, \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\
\'93You will play Glory Hallelujah.\'94\
Gerry took a couple of steps sideways and located the dissident\'97an Agent Jones, Pink Tie series. The agent pulled out a particle pistol and shot into the air. Bad move. Within seconds, he was surrounded by Elvi. The agent went down, crying out, \'93He Touched Me! Precious Lord, Take My Hand! I Shall Not Be Moved!\'94\
\'93Yeah, we\'92ll see about that, brother,\'94 an Elvine said as they bound the Jones with guitar cables and shoved him away.\
Gerry shuddered. Those people always gave him the creeps, and the fact that an agent clone had been compromised made him sad.\
The band lit up a rockabilly tune, much to the relief of the crowd. Gerry mingled, and before long, he\'92d found Clown Squad assembled at the rendezvous.\
Next to his team dressed as Elvi tribute performers, Gerry\'92s impeccable suit made him look more like their cosplaying manager than the nation\'92s next CEO. He checked his watch and considered sending the time and live stream link of Dunhilton\'92s pillowing to Major Midler. Midler should never have been sent on a mission that was sure to fail, but Dunhilton had demanded the right to play with his toys. If the order to deploy the army against its own citizens offended Gerry, he was even more offended Dunhilton hadn\'92t left the mission to PAPER, whose whole purpose was to be deployed against its own citizens. Gerry hoped he\'92d have time to watch Dunhilton\'92s pillowing himself. He\'92d made sure the retiring CEO would meet the Layer from the lowest platform in the city. If the weather was right, Dunhilton would be ankle deep in acidic cloud before he was pushed. Gerry wanted Dunhilton\'92s fall to be not one millisecond shorter than his executioners could extract.\
Gerry and the Clown Squad peeled out of the crowd, and walked south down the middle of the road, past the hover pad, the office park and maintenance buildings.\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
 South of the townhouses, the bass and voices became dull behind the trees. Before they arrived at the lookout they darted quickly into the bush to the right.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Gerry checked a map on his device. An imperfect call of a currawong came from ahead. One of Gerry\'92s squad answered back in kind. Twice. Clown Squad advanced and met Tiger Squad in camp. Gerry scanned the scene; counted the lack  of heads and did the arithmetic.\
\'93How are the bad guys?\'94 Gerry asked.\
\'93All neutralised.\'94\
Gerry nodded while he kept scanning. Most of the gear had made it. So had the rifles. \'93Good job.\'94\
While still alert to the bush, Clown Squad moved in; weapons and magazines changed hands. Rifle slings went beneath capes and magazines disappeared under jumpsuits and into belts.\
Gerry checked one of the weapons, passed it back and looked at the amalgamated team.\
They moved in broken groups. With the townhouses still out of sight to the north, they heard a branch crack. Rifle sights trained on an old man banksia that didn\'92t look like it needed to be told to freeze.\
\'93
\f2\i Che
\f1\i0 !\'94 The banksia said with a young boy\'92s voice.\
\'93Come on down.\'94 Gerry tried to sound friendly while he waved the rifles down.\
Shoes and brown calves appeared first, then a boy\'97Indian or maybe Sri Lankan\'97landed like a gymnast on the mat of the bushland floor.\
\'93Are you here for the concert?\'94 Tikirimanikandarajah Chuglingham asked none of the men in particular.\
\'93Um, yeah,\'94 Gerry answered. \'93How do we look?\'94\
Tiki nodded approval. \'93Like the real thing. You went down to the lookout?\'94\
\'93Yeah,\'94 Gerry confessed. \'93It\'92s pretty down there.\'94\
\'93It is,\'94 Tiki agreed. \'93But heights scare me a bit.\'94\
\'93Me too,\'94 Gerry said. \'93Watcha doin\'92 up a tree?\'94\
\'93Bird watching. It\'92s amazing how many there are around here, not like back in the city. I just saw a gang-gang. And a crimson rosella.\'94\
\'93Oh, they\'92re pretty,\'94 Gerry said. \'93You live here?\'94\
Tiki beamed. \'93We do now.\'94\
\'93What\'92s your name?\'94\
\'93Tikirimanikandarajah Chuglingham. You better just call me Tiki.\'94 Tiki laughed.\
Gerry smiled. \'93Um, I think things up the road are a bit dangerous right now.\'94\
Tiki nodded. \'93Appa called a moment ago. He said I should go inside.\'94\
Gerry nodded. \'93That sounds like good advice.\'94\
Tiki hesitated.\
\'93There\'92ll still be birds tomorrow,\'94 Gerry said.\
\'93Okay. You be careful too,\'94 Tiki said. \'93But you should be all right. Everyone here loves the Elvi.\'94
